.Dd November 17, 2026
.Dt BC 1
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm bc
.Nd arbitrary-precision decimal calculator (bounded POSIX core)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl l
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility reads arithmetic programs from the file operands, or from standard input when no file
operand is present, and writes expression values and
.Ic print
output to standard output.
.Pp
Numbers are arbitrary-precision signed decimals.
The special variable
.Va scale
sets the number of fractional digits used by division and other scale-sensitive operations.
The operators
.Ic + , - , * , / , % , ^
and the relational and logical operators are supported, together with unary
.Ic - 
and
.Ic \&! .
The functions
.Ic sqrt ,
.Ic length
and
.Ic scale
are built in.
.Pp
The language includes scalar variables, integer-indexed arrays, comments, quoted strings,
.Ic if/else ,
.Ic while ,
C-style
.Ic for ,
.Ic break ,
.Ic continue ,
.Ic return ,
.Ic auto
locals and user-defined functions.
A bare expression writes its value; an assignment is silent.
.Ic print
writes its comma-separated arguments without adding a newline.
.Pp
The following option is supported:
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Fl l
Set
.Va scale
to 20 and enable the bounded math library:
.Ic e , l , s , c
and
.Ic a .
.El
.Pp
The
.Va ibase
and
.Va obase
variables are fixed at decimal; assigning another base is an error.
The
.Ic dc
language, GNU extensions, array parameters,
.Ic auto
arrays, the Bessel function
.Ic j
and non-decimal input/output bases are not implemented.
On the target image standard input is read from the console terminal; there is no general process
pipeline handle.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Status 2 indicates invalid usage; status 1 indicates a parse, runtime, unsupported or I/O error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr patch 1 ,
.Xr rsh 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The bounded core follows the POSIX.1-2024
.Nm
language for the implemented statements, operators, functions and the
.Fl l
option.
